@preamble let net = import("net")
connect
Connects to a TCP host and port and returns an I/O endpoint. Use io.read,
io.read_line, io.write, and io.close with the returned endpoint.

connect(host, port)

@unchecked
===
listen
Listens for TCP connections. Port 0 requests an available host port.

listen(host, port)

@unchecked
===
accept
Accepts one connection from a TCP listener and returns an I/O endpoint.

accept(listener)

@unchecked
===
local
Returns the local address as [@address, host, port]. Works with TCP listeners,
TCP endpoints, and UDP sockets.

local(resource)

@unchecked
===
remote
Returns the peer address of a TCP endpoint as [@address, host, port].

remote(endpoint)

@unchecked
===
close
Closes a TCP listener or UDP socket. TCP connection endpoints close through
io.close so all sequential I/O resources share the same lifecycle operation.

close(resource)

@unchecked
===
udp_bind
Binds a UDP socket. Port 0 requests an available host port.

udp_bind(host, port)

@unchecked
===
udp_send
Sends one UDP datagram. Data may be opaque bytes or native @bytes.

udp_send(socket, host, port, data)

@unchecked
===
udp_recv
Receives one complete UDP datagram and returns [@datagram, bytes, host, port].
Datagram boundaries are preserved rather than exposed through io.read.

udp_recv(socket)

@unchecked
